    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "## Steps to reproduce\n\n1. visually select some text:\n   ```\n   a b foo bar\n   x y zub foo\n   ```\n2. search in the visual selection: `\\%Vfoo`\n3. use `cgnX<esc>` to change the first \"foo\" match\n4. `n` does not find the next \"foo\" match, because `cgn` changes the visual marks `'<,'>`\n\n## Expected behavior\n\n`gn` should not change the visual marks.\n\n\nrejected upstream in https://github.com/vim/vim/issues/3402 , but I think we should reconsider it. modifying the visual marks is destructive for no reason.\n\n\nthere is no good reason for this, and it makes `\\%V` + `gn` pretty much useless.",
